#{[{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186049195 :topic/id #uuid "e278bb36-cd42-5d79-a581-bd3a23f09b21" :topic/knowledge-points [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186054622 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186054537 :question/id #uuid "5a566673-4529-57b8-87c9-d5a6118e0398" :question/math-academy-id "e-4082" :question/problem "Express $\\log(3)+4\\log(y)$ as a single logarithm." :question/worked-solution "Using the power rule for logarithms, we can rewrite the second term to obtain\n\n$$\n\\log(3)+4\\log(y)=\\log(3)+\\log(y^4).\n$$\n\nNow, using the product rule for logarithms, we can combine the two terms to get\n\n$$\n\\log(3)+\\log(y^4)=\\log\\left(3y^4\\right).\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\log(3)+4\\log(y)=\\log\\left(3y^4\\right).\n$$"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "4dc94edb-007d-5fe5-b9ca-9ed754adc457" :knowledge-point/question-generator {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186656359 :question-generator/id #uuid "33a4cdc9-e943-50f2-abcd-36b03a740756" :question-generator/script "generators/ea/ea5bf4c583efd1a855456476011fb40fd7bca88355902fc4c7a666a189b4df9f/generator.py"} :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186055334 :question/id #uuid "21c18a11-6bc1-5de0-bcda-f9622b617f6d" :question/math-academy-id "q-47194" :question/problem "What is $\\log (5p^{3})$?"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186055463 :question/id #uuid "c8f2e17a-2817-5868-a9e3-61bbc105efb0" :question/math-academy-id "q-66907" :question/problem "Express $3\\log_{2} (x) + 2\\log_{2} (y)$ as a single logarithm."} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186543524 :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "841f3191-5c7e-5e47-b725-127033cb9a73" :question/math-academy-id "q-79453" :question/problem "If ${log}_{5}⁡(x)=3$ and ${log}_{5}⁡(y)=2$, then ${log}_{5}⁡({x}^{2}{y}^{3})=$" :question/worked-solution "The laws of logarithms are:\n\n- ${log}_{n}⁡(a)+{log}_{n}⁡(b)={log}_{n}⁡(ab)$ - the product rule\n\n- ${log}_{n}⁡(a)-{log}_{n}⁡(b)={log}_{n}⁡\\left(\\frac{a}{b}\\right)$ - the quotient rule\n\n- ${log}_{n}⁡({c}^{m})=m{log}_{n}⁡(c)$ - the power rule\n\nNote that ${log}_{5}⁡({x}^{2}{y}^{3})$ can be rewritten using the product and power rules as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{log}_{5}⁡({x}^{2}{y}^{3}) & ={log}_{5}⁡({x}^{2})+{log}_{5}⁡({y}^{3}) \\\\  & =2{log}_{5}⁡(x)+3{log}_{5}⁡(y)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, since ${log}_{5}⁡(x)=3$ and ${log}_{5}⁡(y)=2$, we conclude that\n\n$$\n2{log}_{5}⁡(x)+3{log}_{5}⁡(y)=2\\cdot 3+3\\cdot 2=12.\n$$\n\nHence\n\n$$\n{log}_{5}⁡({x}^{2}{y}^{3})=12.\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186543531 :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "9796dfce-fb6b-57bd-b442-e9278daaaa20" :question/math-academy-id "q-98925" :question/problem "If ${log}_{2}⁡(x)=k$ and ${log}_{2}⁡(y)=4$, then ${log}_{2}⁡(x{y}^{3})=$" :question/worked-solution "The laws of logarithms are:\n\n- ${log}_{n}⁡(a)+{log}_{n}⁡(b)={log}_{n}⁡(ab)$ - the product rule\n\n- ${log}_{n}⁡(a)-{log}_{n}⁡(b)={log}_{n}⁡\\left(\\frac{a}{b}\\right)$ - the quotient rule\n\n- ${log}_{n}⁡({c}^{m})=m{log}_{n}⁡(c)$ - the power rule\n\nNote that ${log}_{2}⁡(x{y}^{3})$ can be rewritten using the product and power rules as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{log}_{2}⁡(x{y}^{3}) & ={log}_{2}⁡(x)+{log}_{2}⁡({y}^{3}) \\\\  & ={log}_{2}⁡(x)+3{log}_{2}⁡(y)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, since ${log}_{2}⁡(x)=k$ and ${log}_{2}⁡(y)=4,$ we conclude that\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{log}_{2}⁡(x)+3{log}_{2}⁡(y) & =k+3\\cdot 4 \\\\  & =k+12.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n{log}_{2}⁡(x{y}^{3})=k+12.\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186656332 :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "31f7c11e-ddce-52f3-9df1-4a9dbff26cc2" :question/problem "If $\\log_{2}\\left(x\\right)=5$ and $\\log_{2}\\left(y\\right)=4$, then $\\log_{2}\\left(x^{2}y^{3}\\right)=$" :question/worked-solution "The laws of logarithms are:\n\n- $\\log_n(a)+\\log_n(b)=\\log_n(ab)$ - the product rule\n\n- $\\log_n(a)-\\log_n(b)=\\log_n(\\frac{a}{b})$ - the quotient rule\n\n- $\\log_n(c^m)=m\\log_n(c)$ - the power rule\n\nNote that $\\log_{2}\\left(x^{2}y^{3}\\right)$ can be rewritten using the product and power rules as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\log_{2}\\left(x^{2}y^{3}\\right) &=\\log_{2}\\left(x^{2}\\right)+\\log_{2}\\left(y^{3}\\right) \\\\ &=2\\log_{2}\\left(x\\right)+3\\log_{2}\\left(y\\right)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, since $\\log_{2}\\left(x\\right)=5$ and $\\log_{2}\\left(y\\right)=4$, we conclude that\n\n$$\n2\\log_{2}\\left(x\\right)+3\\log_{2}\\left(y\\right)=2\\cdot 5+3\\cdot 4=22.\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\log_{2}\\left(x^{2}y^{3}\\right)=22.\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186656333 :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "382ed20b-83b3-5caf-8ca1-a3baabb17216" :question/problem "If $\\log_{7}\\left(y\\right)=2$ and $\\log_{7}\\left(x\\right)=-2$, then $\\log_{7}\\left(x^{2}y^{3}\\right)=$" :question/worked-solution "The laws of logarithms are:\n\n- $\\log_n(a)+\\log_n(b)=\\log_n(ab)$ - the product rule\n\n- $\\log_n(a)-\\log_n(b)=\\log_n(\\frac{a}{b})$ - the quotient rule\n\n- $\\log_n(c^m)=m\\log_n(c)$ - the power rule\n\nNote that $\\log_{7}\\left(x^{2}y^{3}\\right)$ can be rewritten using the product and power rules as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\log_{7}\\left(x^{2}y^{3}\\right) &=\\log_{7}\\left(x^{2}\\right)+\\log_{7}\\left(y^{3}\\right) \\\\ &=2\\log_{7}\\left(x\\right)+3\\log_{7}\\left(y\\right)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, since $\\log_{7}\\left(y\\right)=2$ and $\\log_{7}\\left(x\\right)=-2$, we conclude that\n\n$$\n2\\log_{7}\\left(x\\right)+3\\log_{7}\\left(y\\right)=2\\cdot (-2)+3\\cdot 2=2.\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\log_{7}\\left(x^{2}y^{3}\\right)=2.\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186656338 :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "6a155d3c-0437-5bdf-afb1-a5fd32a1ba61" :question/problem "If $\\log_{2}\\left(x\\right)=4$ and $\\log_{2}\\left(y\\right)=5$, then $\\log_{2}\\left(x^{5}y^{4}\\right)=$" :question/worked-solution "The laws of logarithms are:\n\n- $\\log_n(a)+\\log_n(b)=\\log_n(ab)$ - the product rule\n\n- $\\log_n(a)-\\log_n(b)=\\log_n(\\frac{a}{b})$ - the quotient rule\n\n- $\\log_n(c^m)=m\\log_n(c)$ - the power rule\n\nNote that $\\log_{2}\\left(x^{5}y^{4}\\right)$ can be rewritten using the product and power rules as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\log_{2}\\left(x^{5}y^{4}\\right) &=\\log_{2}\\left(x^{5}\\right)+\\log_{2}\\left(y^{4}\\right) \\\\ &=5\\log_{2}\\left(x\\right)+4\\log_{2}\\left(y\\right)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, since $\\log_{2}\\left(x\\right)=4$ and $\\log_{2}\\left(y\\right)=5$, we conclude that\n\n$$\n5\\log_{2}\\left(x\\right)+4\\log_{2}\\left(y\\right)=5\\cdot 4+4\\cdot 5=40.\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\log_{2}\\left(x^{5}y^{4}\\right)=40.\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186656344 :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "81ba7524-c55c-55c4-befa-65fc65e1d586" :question/problem "If $\\log_{3}\\left(y\\right)=k$ and $\\log_{3}\\left(x\\right)=5$, then $\\log_{3}\\left(x^{5}y\\right)=$" :question/worked-solution "The laws of logarithms are:\n\n- $\\log_n(a)+\\log_n(b)=\\log_n(ab)$ - the product rule\n\n- $\\log_n(a)-\\log_n(b)=\\log_n(\\frac{a}{b})$ - the quotient rule\n\n- $\\log_n(c^m)=m\\log_n(c)$ - the power rule\n\nNote that $\\log_{3}\\left(x^{5}y\\right)$ can be rewritten using the product and power rules as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\log_{3}\\left(x^{5}y\\right) &=\\log_{3}\\left(x^{5}\\right)+\\log_{3}\\left(y\\right) \\\\ &=5\\log_{3}\\left(x\\right)+\\log_{3}\\left(y\\right)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, since $\\log_{3}\\left(y\\right)=k$ and $\\log_{3}\\left(x\\right)=5$, we conclude that\n\n$$\n5\\log_{3}\\left(x\\right)+\\log_{3}\\left(y\\right)=5\\cdot 5+k=k+25.\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\log_{3}\\left(x^{5}y\\right)=k+25.\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186656355 :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "dbf69bc5-72a9-5eeb-b7a8-7c8cd5feae2d" :question/problem "If $\\log_{10}\\left(y\\right)=6$ and $\\log_{10}\\left(x\\right)=-5$, then $\\log_{10}\\left(x^{3}y^{5}\\right)=$" :question/worked-solution "The laws of logarithms are:\n\n- $\\log_n(a)+\\log_n(b)=\\log_n(ab)$ - the product rule\n\n- $\\log_n(a)-\\log_n(b)=\\log_n(\\frac{a}{b})$ - the quotient rule\n\n- $\\log_n(c^m)=m\\log_n(c)$ - the power rule\n\nNote that $\\log_{10}\\left(x^{3}y^{5}\\right)$ can be rewritten using the product and power rules as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\log_{10}\\left(x^{3}y^{5}\\right) &=\\log_{10}\\left(x^{3}\\right)+\\log_{10}\\left(y^{5}\\right) \\\\ &=3\\log_{10}\\left(x\\right)+5\\log_{10}\\left(y\\right)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, since $\\log_{10}\\left(y\\right)=6$ and $\\log_{10}\\left(x\\right)=-5$, we conclude that\n\n$$\n3\\log_{10}\\left(x\\right)+5\\log_{10}\\left(y\\right)=3\\cdot (-5)+5\\cdot 6=15.\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\log_{10}\\left(x^{3}y^{5}\\right)=15.\n$$"}] :knowledge-point/title "Rewriting an Expression Using the Product and Power Rules of Logarithms"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186054623 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186054538 :question/id #uuid "b0e6a6fc-18fa-57d8-9b4a-2fbd1d7fbb9d" :question/math-academy-id "e-4083" :question/problem "Express $3\\log(x) - 2\\log(5)$ as a single logarithm." :question/worked-solution "Using the power rule for logarithms, we can rewrite both terms of the expression as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}3\\operatorname{log}⁡(x)-2\\operatorname{log}⁡(5) & =\\operatorname{log}⁡({x}^{3})-\\operatorname{log}⁡({5}^{2}) \\\\  & =\\operatorname{log}⁡({x}^{3})-\\operatorname{log}⁡(25)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, using the quotient rule for logarithms, we can combine the two terms to get\n\n$$\n\\log\\left(x^3\\right) - \\log(25) =\\log\\left( \\dfrac{x^3}{25} \\right).\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n3\\log(x) - 2\\log(5) =  \\log\\left( \\dfrac{x^3}{25} \\right).\n$$"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "b2c2df20-2d53-5c2e-a633-9442e118a6b8" :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186054954 :question/id #uuid "b47500fa-8b56-5c05-945b-0311701f0dfb" :question/math-academy-id "q-1100" :question/problem "What is $\\log_{3} \\left(\\frac{x^{2}}{y}\\right)$?"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186054958 :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "32c70eb9-aeaf-58d7-a63a-347e864e24f1" :question/math-academy-id "q-1109" :question/problem "Express $2\\log_{2} (x) - 3\\log_{2} (y)$ as a single logarithm."} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186543487 :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "2c8cf324-472b-5836-8692-8d44663200a5" :question/math-academy-id "q-1101" :question/problem "$\\operatorname{log}⁡\\left(\\frac{3y}{\\sqrt{x}}\\right)=$" :question/worked-solution "The laws of logarithms are:\n\n- ${log}_{n}⁡(a)+{log}_{n}⁡(b)={log}_{n}⁡(ab)$ - the product rule\n\n- ${log}_{n}⁡(a)-{log}_{n}⁡(b)={log}_{n}⁡\\left(\\frac{a}{b}\\right)$ - the quotient rule\n\n- ${log}_{n}⁡({c}^{m})=m{log}_{n}⁡(c)$ - the power rule\n\nUsing the quotient rule and then the power rule for logarithms, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{log}⁡\\left(\\frac{3y}{\\sqrt{x}}\\right) & =\\operatorname{log}⁡(3y)-\\operatorname{log}⁡(\\sqrt{x}) \\\\  & =\\operatorname{log}⁡(3y)-\\operatorname{log}⁡({x}^{1/2}) \\\\  & =\\operatorname{log}⁡(3y)-\\frac{1}{2}\\operatorname{log}⁡(x).\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186543498 :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "49c1090f-0d14-5ccb-9c30-a8b9c4fffd87" :question/math-academy-id "q-79403" :question/problem "Express $4\\operatorname{ln}⁡(x)-2\\operatorname{ln}⁡(y)$ as a single logarithm." :question/worked-solution "The laws of logarithms are:\n\n- ${log}_{n}⁡(a)+{log}_{n}⁡(b)={log}_{n}⁡(ab)$ - the product rule\n\n- ${log}_{n}⁡(a)-{log}_{n}⁡(b)={log}_{n}⁡\\left(\\frac{a}{b}\\right)$ - the quotient rule\n\n- ${log}_{n}⁡({c}^{m})=m{log}_{n}⁡(c)$ - the power rule\n\nUsing the power rule and then the quotient rule for logarithms, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}4\\operatorname{ln}⁡(x)-2\\operatorname{ln}⁡(y) & =\\operatorname{ln}⁡({x}^{4})-\\operatorname{ln}⁡({y}^{2}) \\\\  & =\\operatorname{ln}⁡\\left(\\frac{{x}^{4}}{{y}^{2}}\\right).\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186543519 :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "7a291781-59c8-57ab-aafe-d8f0841ec267" :question/math-academy-id "q-47198" :question/problem "$\\operatorname{ln}⁡\\left(\\frac{{z}^{6}}{13}\\right)=$" :question/worked-solution "The laws of logarithms are:\n\n- ${log}_{n}⁡(a)+{log}_{n}⁡(b)={log}_{n}⁡(ab)$ - the product rule\n\n- ${log}_{n}⁡(a)-{log}_{n}⁡(b)={log}_{n}⁡\\left(\\frac{a}{b}\\right)$ - the quotient rule\n\n- ${log}_{n}⁡({c}^{m})=m{log}_{n}⁡(c)$ - the power rule\n\nUsing the quotient quotient and then the power rule for logarithms, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{ln}⁡\\left(\\frac{{z}^{6}}{13}\\right) & =\\operatorname{ln}⁡({z}^{6})-\\operatorname{ln}⁡(13) \\\\  & =6\\operatorname{ln}⁡(z)-\\operatorname{ln}⁡(13).\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186543541 :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "a8ef19a1-f2fa-5f29-a4ae-a1f3f6bb227d" :question/math-academy-id "q-1110" :question/problem "Express $\\frac{1}{2}{log}_{5}⁡(x)-5{log}_{5}⁡(y)$ as a single logarithm." :question/worked-solution "The laws of logarithms are:\n\n- ${log}_{n}⁡(a)+{log}_{n}⁡(b)={log}_{n}⁡(ab)$ - the product rule\n\n- ${log}_{n}⁡(a)-{log}_{n}⁡(b)={log}_{n}⁡\\left(\\frac{a}{b}\\right)$ - the quotient rule\n\n- ${log}_{n}⁡({c}^{m})=m{log}_{n}⁡(c)$ - the power rule\n\nUsing the power rule and then the quotient rule for logarithms, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1}{2}{log}_{5}⁡(x)-5{log}_{5}⁡(y) & ={log}_{5}⁡({x}^{1/2})-{log}_{5}⁡({y}^{5}) \\\\  & ={log}_{5}⁡(\\sqrt{x})-{log}_{5}⁡({y}^{5}) \\\\  & ={log}_{5}⁡\\left(\\frac{\\sqrt{x}}{{y}^{5}}\\right).\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186543577 :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "e70f2288-3b25-5d12-a8c5-361a6e66e0f3" :question/math-academy-id "q-100243" :question/problem "Express $\\frac{1}{3}{log}_{4}⁡(x)-{log}_{4}⁡(y)$ as a single logarithm." :question/worked-solution "The laws of logarithms are:\n\n- ${log}_{n}⁡(a)+{log}_{n}⁡(b)={log}_{n}⁡(ab)$ - the product rule\n\n- ${log}_{n}⁡(a)-{log}_{n}⁡(b)={log}_{n}⁡\\left(\\frac{a}{b}\\right)$ - the quotient rule\n\n- ${log}_{n}⁡({c}^{m})=m{log}_{n}⁡(c)$ - the power rule\n\nUsing the power rule and then the quotient rule for logarithms, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1}{3}{log}_{4}⁡(x)-{log}_{4}⁡(y) & ={log}_{4}⁡({x}^{1/3})-{log}_{4}⁡(y) \\\\  & ={log}_{4}⁡(\\sqrt[3]{x})-{log}_{4}⁡(y) \\\\  & ={log}_{4}⁡\\left(\\frac{\\sqrt[3]{x}}{y}\\right).\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186600834 :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "e2c8e2c9-e71a-5532-ae52-1a56bdbb7947" :question/math-academy-id "q-47196" :question/problem "Express ${log}_{2}⁡(5)-7{log}_{2}⁡(t)$ as a single logarithm." :question/worked-solution "The laws of logarithms are:\n\n- ${log}_{n}⁡(a)+{log}_{n}⁡(b)={log}_{n}⁡(ab)$ - the product rule\n\n- ${log}_{n}⁡(a)-{log}_{n}⁡(b)={log}_{n}⁡\\left(\\frac{a}{b}\\right)$ - the quotient rule\n\n- ${log}_{n}⁡({c}^{m})=m{log}_{n}⁡(c)$ - the power rule\n\nUsing the power rule and then the quotient rule for logarithms, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{log}_{2}⁡(5)-7{log}_{2}⁡(t) & ={log}_{2}⁡(5)-\\operatorname{log}⁡({t}^{7}) \\\\  & ={log}_{2}⁡\\left(\\frac{5}{{t}^{7}}\\right).\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] :knowledge-point/title "Rewriting an Expression Using the Quotient and Power Rules of Logarithms"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186054624 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186054539 :question/id #uuid "c6e57845-614f-5be1-8551-d85d4ffc490e" :question/math-academy-id "e-4085" :question/problem "Express $\\ln(2)+\\ln(x)-\\ln(y)$ as a single logarithm." :question/worked-solution "Using the product rule for natural logarithms, we can combine the first two terms of the given expression as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{ln}⁡(2)+\\operatorname{ln}⁡(x)-\\operatorname{ln}⁡(y)=\\operatorname{ln}⁡(2x)-\\operatorname{ln}⁡(y)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, using the quotient rule for natural logarithms, we get\n\n$$\n\\ln(2x)-\\ln(y) = \\ln \\left( \\dfrac{2x}{y} \\right).\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\ln(2)+\\ln(x)-\\ln(y) = \\ln\\left(\\dfrac{2x}{y}\\right).\n$$"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "22382eaf-2654-5c65-b7ba-ac71015e84b8" :knowledge-point/question-generator {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186608313 :question-generator/id #uuid "9fc788d9-158c-549b-85ca-188005b37a00" :question-generator/script "generators/2b/2b85d4e47d7b57e56322672f222d5e2f3d395708c5cd3ebc3222ab720c56644e/generator.py"} :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186055335 :question/id #uuid "b1718e3f-bca9-5717-b799-1f5602a61989" :question/math-academy-id "q-47200" :question/problem "Express $\\log (3) + \\log (y) - \\log (z)$ as a single logarithm."} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186055336 :question/id #uuid "6eadfca1-983f-5ae1-b413-9d183209a25c" :question/math-academy-id "q-47203" :question/problem "What is $\\log_{3} \\left(\\frac{9x}{2}\\right)$?"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186543502 :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "4d345e50-4921-588f-ae74-039f20e6e137" :question/math-academy-id "q-100242" :question/problem "$\\operatorname{ln}⁡\\left(\\frac{ex}{5}\\right)=$" :question/worked-solution "The laws of logarithms are:\n\n- ${log}_{n}⁡(a)+{log}_{n}⁡(b)={log}_{n}⁡(ab)$ - the product rule\n\n- ${log}_{n}⁡(a)-{log}_{n}⁡(b)={log}_{n}⁡\\left(\\frac{a}{b}\\right)$ - the quotient rule\n\n- ${log}_{n}⁡({c}^{m})=m{log}_{n}⁡(c)$ - the power rule\n\nUsing the quotient rule and then the product rule for logarithms, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{ln}⁡\\left(\\frac{ex}{5}\\right) & =\\operatorname{ln}⁡(ex)-\\operatorname{ln}⁡(5) \\\\  & =\\operatorname{ln}⁡(e)+\\operatorname{ln}⁡(x)-\\operatorname{ln}⁡(5) \\\\  & =1+\\operatorname{ln}⁡(x)-\\operatorname{ln}⁡(5).\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186543526 :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "8b91269e-ac3e-5858-87ab-575f9e7b0df8" :question/math-academy-id "q-66781" :question/problem "${log}_{3}⁡\\left(\\frac{5x}{27}\\right)=$" :question/worked-solution "The laws of logarithms are:\n\n- ${log}_{n}⁡(a)+{log}_{n}⁡(b)={log}_{n}⁡(ab)$ - the product rule\n\n- ${log}_{n}⁡(a)-{log}_{n}⁡(b)={log}_{n}⁡\\left(\\frac{a}{b}\\right)$ - the quotient rule\n\n- ${log}_{n}⁡({c}^{m})=m{log}_{n}⁡(c)$ - the power rule\n\nUsing the quotient rule and then the product rule for logarithms, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{log}_{3}⁡\\left(\\frac{5x}{27}\\right) & ={log}_{3}⁡(5x)-{log}_{3}⁡(27) \\\\  & ={log}_{3}⁡(5)+{log}_{3}⁡(x)-{log}_{3}⁡({3}^{3}) \\\\  & ={log}_{3}⁡(5)+{log}_{3}⁡(x)-3.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186608285 :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "38389750-0e07-56c4-a5aa-678aeeb21e2e" :question/problem "Express $\\ln\\left(q\\right)-\\ln\\left(p\\right)-\\ln\\left(9\\right)$ as a single logarithm. Assume $q>0$ and $p>0$." :question/worked-solution "Using the product rule, combine the logarithms with the same sign. Then use the quotient rule to subtract the denominator logarithm:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\ln\\left(q\\right)-\\ln\\left(p\\right)-\\ln\\left(9\\right) &= \\ln\\left(q\\right)-\\ln\\left(p\\cdot 9\\right) \\\\ &= \\ln\\left(\\frac{q}{9\\cdot p}\\right).\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186608296 :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "6d58a890-eb4c-5e93-b685-d4836d49f5f1" :question/problem "Express $-\\ln\\left(5\\right)-\\ln\\left(z\\right)+\\ln\\left(x\\right)$ as a single logarithm. Assume $x>0$ and $z>0$." :question/worked-solution "Using the product rule, combine the logarithms with the same sign. Then use the quotient rule to subtract the denominator logarithm:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}-\\ln\\left(5\\right)-\\ln\\left(z\\right)+\\ln\\left(x\\right) &= \\ln\\left(x\\right)-\\ln\\left(5\\cdot z\\right) \\\\ &= \\ln\\left(\\frac{x}{5\\cdot z}\\right).\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186608299 :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "7adce857-91b2-549c-8c73-9e5b68d8232a" :question/problem "Assume $q>0$.\n\n$\\ln\\left(\\frac{q\\cdot 2}{e}\\right)=$" :question/worked-solution "Using the quotient rule and then the product rule for logarithms, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\ln\\left(\\frac{q\\cdot 2}{e}\\right) &= \\ln\\left(q\\cdot 2\\right)-\\ln\\left(e\\right) \\\\ &= -\\ln\\left(e\\right)+\\ln\\left(2\\right)+\\ln\\left(q\\right) \\\\ &= -1+\\ln\\left(2\\right)+\\ln\\left(q\\right).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nHere $\\ln\\left(e\\right)=1$."} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186608300 :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "81376a3f-1801-57b2-98c1-d7cced6f0f6c" :question/problem "Assume $p>0$.\n\n$\\ln\\left(\\frac{p}{e\\cdot 3}\\right)=$" :question/worked-solution "Using the quotient rule and then the product rule for logarithms, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\ln\\left(\\frac{p}{e\\cdot 3}\\right) &= \\ln\\left(p\\right)-\\ln\\left(e\\cdot 3\\right) \\\\ &= -\\ln\\left(e\\right)-\\ln\\left(3\\right)+\\ln\\left(p\\right) \\\\ &= -1-\\ln\\left(3\\right)+\\ln\\left(p\\right).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nHere $\\ln\\left(e\\right)=1$."} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186608308 :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "b3d53eb1-c5f0-51f3-be9a-3c9eb88f1d03" :question/problem "Express $\\log\\left(9\\right)+\\log\\left(z\\right)-\\log\\left(x\\right)$ as a single logarithm. Assume $z>0$ and $x>0$." :question/worked-solution "Using the product rule, combine the logarithms with the same sign. Then use the quotient rule to subtract the denominator logarithm:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\log\\left(9\\right)+\\log\\left(z\\right)-\\log\\left(x\\right) &= \\log\\left(9\\cdot z\\right)-\\log\\left(x\\right) \\\\ &= \\log\\left(\\frac{9\\cdot z}{x}\\right).\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] :knowledge-point/title "Rewriting an Expression Using the Product and Quotient Rules of Logarithms"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186054625 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186054542 :question/id #uuid "de20802c-6a7e-5d77-b2ce-26f98dc68b4e" :question/math-academy-id "e-4095" :question/problem "Express $3\\log(2)+\\log(x)-\\log(4)$ as a single logarithm." :question/worked-solution "Using the power rule for logarithms, we can rewrite the first term of the given expression as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}3\\operatorname{log}⁡(2)+\\operatorname{log}⁡(x)-\\operatorname{log}⁡(4) & =\\operatorname{log}⁡({2}^{3})+\\operatorname{log}⁡(x)-\\operatorname{log}⁡(4) \\\\  & =\\operatorname{log}⁡(8)+\\operatorname{log}⁡(x)-\\operatorname{log}⁡(4)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, using the product rule for logarithms, we can combine the first two terms to obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{log}⁡(8)+\\operatorname{log}⁡(x)-\\operatorname{log}⁡(4)=\\operatorname{log}⁡(8x)-\\operatorname{log}⁡(4).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, using the quotient rule for logarithms, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{log}⁡(8x)-\\operatorname{log}⁡(4) & =\\operatorname{log}⁡\\left(\\frac{8x}{4}\\right) \\\\  & =\\operatorname{log}⁡(2x).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n3\\log(2)+\\log(x)-\\log(4) = \\log\\left(2x\\right).\n$$"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "5dd9426d-8ce3-564d-85c5-4ae82289325d" :knowledge-point/question-generator {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186608381 :question-generator/id #uuid "4ceb511d-7e72-5ab3-b790-cf8a4b698bbb" :question-generator/script "generators/d2/d22bc61dbe5d5e954a0b7cce9d800e9795e2ef28137adc1a72a2db643e6feafd/generator.py"} :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186054957 :question/id #uuid "d039db42-48a7-5fd4-9b98-e0ab1ba34d9c" :question/math-academy-id "q-1102" :question/problem "What is $\\log \\left(\\frac{xy^{3}}{z}\\right)$?"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186054961 :question/id #uuid "3e2b66a0-666e-508e-a538-22e2d9fb9285" :question/math-academy-id "q-1111" :question/problem "Express $3\\ln (x) + 2\\ln (y) - \\frac{1}{2}\\ln (z)$ as a single logarithm."} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186543491 :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "34847574-efca-5670-8924-e70a3bcc31ce" :question/math-academy-id "q-66923" :question/problem "${log}_{2}⁡\\left(\\frac{\\sqrt{x}{y}^{2}}{z}\\right)=$" :question/worked-solution "The laws of logarithms are:\n\n- ${log}_{n}⁡(a)+{log}_{n}⁡(b)={log}_{n}⁡(ab)$ - the product rule\n\n- ${log}_{n}⁡(a)-{log}_{n}⁡(b)={log}_{n}⁡\\left(\\frac{a}{b}\\right)$ - the quotient rule\n\n- ${log}_{n}⁡({c}^{m})=m{log}_{n}⁡(c)$ - the power rule\n\nUsing the quotient rule for logarithms, we get\n\n$$\n{log}_{2}⁡\\left(\\frac{\\sqrt{x}{y}^{2}}{z}\\right)={log}_{2}⁡(\\sqrt{x}{y}^{2})-{log}_{2}⁡(z).\n$$\n\nNow, using the product rule for logarithms, we obtain\n\n$$\n{log}_{2}⁡(\\sqrt{x}{y}^{2})-{log}_{2}⁡(z)={log}_{2}⁡(\\sqrt{x})+{log}_{2}⁡({y}^{2})-{log}_{2}⁡(z).\n$$\n\nFinally, using the power rule for logarithms, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{log}_{2}⁡(\\sqrt{x})+{log}_{2}⁡({y}^{2})-{log}_{2}⁡(z) & ={log}_{2}⁡({x}^{1/2})+{log}_{2}⁡({y}^{2})-{log}_{2}⁡(z) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}{log}_{2}⁡(x)+2{log}_{2}⁡(y)-{log}_{2}⁡(z).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore\n\n$$\n{log}_{2}⁡\\left(\\frac{\\sqrt{x}{y}^{2}}{z}\\right)=\\frac{1}{2}{log}_{2}⁡(x)+2{log}_{2}⁡(y)-{log}_{2}⁡(z).\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186543573 :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "e3b649a8-d282-53b7-b8c8-e883fd47c0b9" :question/math-academy-id "q-79406" :question/problem "${log}_{3}⁡\\left(\\frac{{x}^{2}{y}^{3}}{\\sqrt{z}}\\right)=$" :question/worked-solution "The laws of logarithms are:\n\n- ${log}_{n}⁡(a)+{log}_{n}⁡(b)={log}_{n}⁡(ab)$ - the product rule\n\n- ${log}_{n}⁡(a)-{log}_{n}⁡(b)={log}_{n}⁡\\left(\\frac{a}{b}\\right)$ - the quotient rule\n\n- ${log}_{n}⁡({c}^{m})=m{log}_{n}⁡(c)$ - the power rule\n\nUsing the quotient rule for logarithms, we get:\n\n$$\n{log}_{3}⁡\\left(\\frac{{x}^{2}{y}^{3}}{\\sqrt{z}}\\right)={log}_{3}⁡({x}^{2}{y}^{3})-{log}_{3}⁡(\\sqrt{z})\n$$\n\nNow, using the product rule for logarithms, we obtain\n\n$$\n{log}_{3}⁡({x}^{2}{y}^{3})-{log}_{3}⁡(\\sqrt{z})={log}_{3}⁡({x}^{2})+{log}_{3}⁡({y}^{3})-{log}_{3}⁡(\\sqrt{z}).\n$$\n\nFinally, using the power rule for logarithms, we have\n\n$$\n{log}_{3}⁡({x}^{2})+{log}_{3}⁡({y}^{3})-{log}_{3}⁡(\\sqrt{z})=2{log}_{3}⁡(x)+3{log}_{3}⁡(y)-\\frac{1}{2}{log}_{3}⁡(z).\n$$\n\nTherefore\n\n$$\n{log}_{3}⁡\\left(\\frac{{x}^{2}{y}^{3}}{\\sqrt{z}}\\right)=2{log}_{3}⁡(x)+3{log}_{3}⁡(y)-\\frac{1}{2}{log}_{3}⁡(z).\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186608363 :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "8aed1f5f-94a6-53e0-bffe-9da4a87b67b6" :question/problem "Assume $x,y,z>0$.\n\n$\\log_{5}\\left(\\frac{y^{2}}{\\sqrt{x}z^{4}}\\right)=$" :question/worked-solution "The laws of logarithms are:\n\n- $\\log_{n}\\left(a\\right)+\\log_{n}\\left(b\\right)=\\log_{n}\\left(ab\\right)$ — the product rule\n\n- $\\log_{n}\\left(a\\right)-\\log_{n}\\left(b\\right)=\\log_{n}\\left(\\frac{a}{b}\\right)$ — the quotient rule\n\n- $\\log_{n}\\left(c^{m}\\right)=m\\log_{n}\\left(c\\right)$ — the power rule\n\nUsing the quotient rule for logarithms, we get:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}& \\log_{5}\\left(\\frac{y^{2}}{\\sqrt{x}z^{4}}\\right) \\\\ &= \\log_{5}\\left(y^{2}\\right)-\\log_{5}\\left(\\sqrt{x}z^{4}\\right)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, using the product rule for logarithms, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}& \\log_{5}\\left(y^{2}\\right)-\\log_{5}\\left(\\sqrt{x}z^{4}\\right) \\\\ &= -\\log_{5}\\left(\\sqrt{x}\\right)-\\log_{5}\\left(z^{4}\\right)+\\log_{5}\\left(y^{2}\\right)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, using the power rule for logarithms (a square root is a power of one half), we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}& -\\log_{5}\\left(\\sqrt{x}\\right)-\\log_{5}\\left(z^{4}\\right)+\\log_{5}\\left(y^{2}\\right) \\\\ &= -\\frac{1}{2}\\log_{5}\\left(x\\right)-4\\log_{5}\\left(z\\right)+2\\log_{5}\\left(y\\right)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}& \\log_{5}\\left(\\frac{y^{2}}{\\sqrt{x}z^{4}}\\right) \\\\ &= -\\frac{1}{2}\\log_{5}\\left(x\\right)-4\\log_{5}\\left(z\\right)+2\\log_{5}\\left(y\\right)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThese identities hold for $x,y,z>0$."} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186608364 :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "91336788-d4fe-55bb-b76d-d8f6b2ce687c" :question/problem "Assume $x,y,z>0$.\n\nExpress $3\\log_{2}\\left(y\\right)+\\frac{1}{2}\\log_{2}\\left(z\\right)-4\\log_{2}\\left(x\\right)$ as a single logarithm." :question/worked-solution "Using the power rule for logarithms, we can rewrite the terms as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}& 3\\log_{2}\\left(y\\right)+\\frac{1}{2}\\log_{2}\\left(z\\right)-4\\log_{2}\\left(x\\right) \\\\ &= \\log_{2}\\left(y^{3}\\right)+\\log_{2}\\left(\\sqrt{z}\\right)-\\log_{2}\\left(x^{4}\\right)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, using the product rule for logarithms, we combine the factors with the same sign:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}& \\log_{2}\\left(y^{3}\\right)+\\log_{2}\\left(\\sqrt{z}\\right)-\\log_{2}\\left(x^{4}\\right) \\\\ &= \\log_{2}\\left(y^{3}\\sqrt{z}\\right)-\\log_{2}\\left(x^{4}\\right)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, using the quotient rule for logarithms, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}& \\log_{2}\\left(y^{3}\\sqrt{z}\\right)-\\log_{2}\\left(x^{4}\\right) \\\\ &= \\log_{2}\\left(\\frac{y^{3}\\sqrt{z}}{x^{4}}\\right)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}& 3\\log_{2}\\left(y\\right)+\\frac{1}{2}\\log_{2}\\left(z\\right)-4\\log_{2}\\left(x\\right) \\\\ &= \\log_{2}\\left(\\frac{y^{3}\\sqrt{z}}{x^{4}}\\right)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThese identities hold for $x,y,z>0$."} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186608368 :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "b34f9130-5590-5e81-9633-557267f7645f" :question/problem "Assume $x,y,z>0$.\n\n$\\log\\left(\\frac{y^{3}}{\\sqrt{x}z^{5}}\\right)=$" :question/worked-solution "The laws of logarithms are:\n\n- $\\log_{n}\\left(a\\right)+\\log_{n}\\left(b\\right)=\\log_{n}\\left(ab\\right)$ — the product rule\n\n- $\\log_{n}\\left(a\\right)-\\log_{n}\\left(b\\right)=\\log_{n}\\left(\\frac{a}{b}\\right)$ — the quotient rule\n\n- $\\log_{n}\\left(c^{m}\\right)=m\\log_{n}\\left(c\\right)$ — the power rule\n\nUsing the quotient rule for logarithms, we get:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}& \\log\\left(\\frac{y^{3}}{\\sqrt{x}z^{5}}\\right) \\\\ &= \\log\\left(y^{3}\\right)-\\log\\left(\\sqrt{x}z^{5}\\right)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, using the product rule for logarithms, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}& \\log\\left(y^{3}\\right)-\\log\\left(\\sqrt{x}z^{5}\\right) \\\\ &= -\\log\\left(z^{5}\\right)-\\log\\left(\\sqrt{x}\\right)+\\log\\left(y^{3}\\right)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, using the power rule for logarithms (a square root is a power of one half), we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}& -\\log\\left(z^{5}\\right)-\\log\\left(\\sqrt{x}\\right)+\\log\\left(y^{3}\\right) \\\\ &= -5\\log\\left(z\\right)-\\frac{1}{2}\\log\\left(x\\right)+3\\log\\left(y\\right)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}& \\log\\left(\\frac{y^{3}}{\\sqrt{x}z^{5}}\\right) \\\\ &= -5\\log\\left(z\\right)-\\frac{1}{2}\\log\\left(x\\right)+3\\log\\left(y\\right)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThese identities hold for $x,y,z>0$."} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186608371 :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "baf39665-b368-5de7-b858-419c7ef09632" :question/problem "Assume $x>0$.\n\nExpress $-3\\log_{3}\\left(2\\right)+\\log_{3}\\left(4\\right)-\\log_{3}\\left(x\\right)$ as a single logarithm." :question/worked-solution "Using the power rule for logarithms, rewrite the powered numerical term and evaluate the power:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}& -3\\log_{3}\\left(2\\right)+\\log_{3}\\left(4\\right)-\\log_{3}\\left(x\\right) \\\\ &= -\\log_{3}\\left(2^{3}\\right)+\\log_{3}\\left(4\\right)-\\log_{3}\\left(x\\right) \\\\ &= -\\log_{3}\\left(8\\right)+\\log_{3}\\left(4\\right)-\\log_{3}\\left(x\\right)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, using the product rule for logarithms, combine the factors with the same sign:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}& -\\log_{3}\\left(8\\right)+\\log_{3}\\left(4\\right)-\\log_{3}\\left(x\\right) \\\\ &= \\log_{3}\\left(4\\right)-\\log_{3}\\left(8x\\right)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, using the quotient rule for logarithms and simplifying the numerical factor, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}& \\log_{3}\\left(4\\right)-\\log_{3}\\left(8x\\right) \\\\ &= \\log_{3}\\left(\\frac{4}{8x}\\right) \\\\ &= \\log_{3}\\left(\\frac{1}{2x}\\right)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}& -3\\log_{3}\\left(2\\right)+\\log_{3}\\left(4\\right)-\\log_{3}\\left(x\\right) \\\\ &= \\log_{3}\\left(\\frac{1}{2x}\\right)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThese identities hold for $x>0$."} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186608372 :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "bdffb806-9aac-5618-883f-6ba573ab4d59" :question/problem "Assume $x,y,z>0$.\n\nExpress $-2\\log_{2}\\left(x\\right)+\\frac{1}{2}\\log_{2}\\left(z\\right)+3\\log_{2}\\left(y\\right)$ as a single logarithm." :question/worked-solution "Using the power rule for logarithms, we can rewrite the terms as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}& -2\\log_{2}\\left(x\\right)+\\frac{1}{2}\\log_{2}\\left(z\\right)+3\\log_{2}\\left(y\\right) \\\\ &= -\\log_{2}\\left(x^{2}\\right)+\\log_{2}\\left(\\sqrt{z}\\right)+\\log_{2}\\left(y^{3}\\right)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, using the product rule for logarithms, we combine the factors with the same sign:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}& -\\log_{2}\\left(x^{2}\\right)+\\log_{2}\\left(\\sqrt{z}\\right)+\\log_{2}\\left(y^{3}\\right) \\\\ &= \\log_{2}\\left(y^{3}\\sqrt{z}\\right)-\\log_{2}\\left(x^{2}\\right)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, using the quotient rule for logarithms, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}& \\log_{2}\\left(y^{3}\\sqrt{z}\\right)-\\log_{2}\\left(x^{2}\\right) \\\\ &= \\log_{2}\\left(\\frac{y^{3}\\sqrt{z}}{x^{2}}\\right)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}& -2\\log_{2}\\left(x\\right)+\\frac{1}{2}\\log_{2}\\left(z\\right)+3\\log_{2}\\left(y\\right) \\\\ &= \\log_{2}\\left(\\frac{y^{3}\\sqrt{z}}{x^{2}}\\right)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThese identities hold for $x,y,z>0$."}] :knowledge-point/title "Rewriting an Expression Using the Product, Quotient, and Power Rules of Logarithms"}] :topic/math-academy-id 30 :topic/next [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186048140} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186048346} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186048553} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186049182} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186049443}] :topic/title "Combining the Laws of Logarithms"}]}
